  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nFor SCP/SFTP, curl verifies the server's host key once, during the initial connect state machine,\nin `ssh_check_fingerprint()` — which reads `CURLOPT_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256` / `_MD5`, the\n`CURLOPT_SSH_KNOWNHOSTS` file, and the `CURLOPT_SSH_HOSTKEYFUNCTION` callback. That function has\nexactly one caller, `ssh_state_hostkey()` (`lib/vssh/libssh2.c:1461`), reached only on a fresh\nconnect.\n\nThe SSH connection-reuse gate, `ssh_config_matches()`, compares only the client identity key files:\n\n```c\n/* lib/url.c:569-578 */\nstatic bool ssh_config_matches(struct connectdata *one,\n                               struct connectdata *two)\n{\n  struct ssh_conn *sshc1, *sshc2;\n  sshc1 = Curl_conn_meta_get(one, CURL_META_SSH_CONN);\n  sshc2 = Curl_conn_meta_get(two, CURL_META_SSH_CONN);\n  return sshc1 && sshc2 && Curl_safecmp(sshc1->priv_key, sshc2->priv_key) &&\n         Curl_safecmp(sshc1->pub_key, sshc2->pub_key);\n}\n```\n\nNone of the host-key verification inputs are part of this comparison (`git grep` for\n`SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256|_MD5|SSH_KNOWNHOSTS|ssh_hostkeyfunc` in `lib/url.c` returns 0 hits; the\nonly `ssh_auth_types` hit at `url.c:375` is default initialization, not a reuse comparison).\n\nFinally, the reuse/attach operation performs no re-verification — it only re-points libssh2's\nabstract pointer at the new easy handle:\n\n```c\n/* lib/vssh/libssh2.c:3910-3922 */\nstatic void ssh_attach(struct Curl_easy *data, struct connectdata *conn)\n{\n  ...\n    if(sshc && sshc->ssh_session) {\n      void **abstract = libssh2_session_abstract(sshc->ssh_session);\n      *abstract = data;   /* no ssh_check_fingerprint() here */\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\nHost-key verification policy is treated as a connect-time action, not as part of the connection's\nsecurity identity. Because the verification inputs are absent from `ssh_config_matches()` and\n`ssh_attach()` does not re-check, a connection established under one host-key policy can be handed to\na later transfer that requested a stricter policy, and the stricter check never runs.\n\n## Attacker model / reachability\n\nTwo SCP/SFTP transfers in one process over a shared connection pool (one easy handle reused, a multi\nhandle, or `CURLSH` with `CURL_LOCK_DATA_CONNECT`), to the same host/port/user with the same identity\nkey files, where:\n- transfer #1 uses weak or no host-key verification (no known_hosts, no pin) — with no known_hosts\n  configured, libssh2 accepts the presented host key (`lib/vssh/libssh2.c:319-322`); and\n- transfer #2 configures a host-key pin (`CURLOPT_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256`), a `known_hosts`\n  file, or a host-key callback.\n\n`ssh_config_matches()` returns TRUE (identity key files match), so transfer #2 reuses transfer #1's\nconnection and `ssh_check_fingerprint()` is never called for it. If a man-in-the-middle substituted\nthe server during transfer #1 (accepted because #1 did not verify), transfer #2 — which demanded a\npin precisely to detect that — proceeds on the attacker-controlled connection. The host-key pin /\nknown_hosts / callback is silently bypassed.\n\n## Live PoC (confirmed end-to-end)\n\nConfirmed against curl built from this commit with the libssh2 backend (libssh2 1.11.2_DEV, built\nfrom source) talking to a paramiko SFTP server with a fixed host key. `attachments/ssh_client.c`\nperforms two SFTP GETs on **one** easy handle (shared pool): transfer #1 with no host-key\nverification; transfer #2 with a deliberately **wrong** `CURLOPT_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256` pin.\n`attachments/poc_ssh_hostkey.py` drives it and compares the reuse case against a `CURLOPT_FRESH_CONNECT`\ncontrol. Captured output (`attachments/ssh_hostkey_transcript.txt`):\n\n```\n################ EXPLOIT (fresh2=0): #2 reuses #1's pooled SFTP connection ################\n[client] transfer 1  pin=(none)   fresh=0 -> rc=0 (No error)\n[client] transfer 2  pin=AAAA...= fresh=0 -> rc=0 (No error)\n  RESULT rc1=0 rc2=0\n  >>> wrong host-key pin was NOT enforced on the reused connection\n\n################ CONTROL (fresh2=1): #2 forced onto a FRESH connection ################\n[client] transfer 1  pin=(none)   fresh=0 -> rc=0 (No error)\n[client] transfer 2  pin=AAAA...= fresh=1 -> rc=60 (SSH remote key was not OK)\n  RESULT rc1=0 rc2=60\n  >>> on a fresh connection the wrong pin IS enforced\n```\n\nThe exact same wrong SHA256 pin that correctly fails verification on a fresh connection (rc=60,\n`CURLE_PEER_FAILED_VERIFICATION`) is silently accepted when transfer #2 reuses transfer #1's\nconnection (rc=0). That is the host-key verification bypass, demonstrated directly.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```\n# build libssh2 (from source) and curl --with-libssh2, then:\ngcc -I include attachments/ssh_client.c lib/.libs/libcurl.a \\\n    -L<libssh2-prefix>/lib -lssh2 -lssl -lcrypto -lbrotlidec -lbrotlicommon -lz -o ssh_client\npip install paramiko\npython3 -I attachments/poc_ssh_hostkey.py   # edit SCRATCH path at top if needed\n```\n\nThe pure-source verifier (`attachments/verify.sh` / `verify-output.txt`) remains included: it shows\nthe single fingerprint-check call site, the fields compared for reuse, and the no-op attach path.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd the host-key verification inputs to the SSH reuse comparison — `STRING_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256`,\n`STRING_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_MD5`, `STRING_SSH_KNOWNHOSTS`, and the presence/identity of\n`ssh_hostkeyfunc` — in `ssh_config_matches()` (mirroring how `priv_key`/`pub_key` were added for\nCVE-2022-27782), or re-run `ssh_check_fingerprint()` on reuse in `ssh_attach()`. A connection must\nnot be reused by a transfer whose host-key policy is stricter than the one the connection was\nestablished under.\n\n## Impact\n\n## Impact\n\nA transfer that explicitly requests SSH host-key verification — a SHA256/MD5 host-key pin, a\n`known_hosts` file, or a host-key callback — can have that verification silently skipped when it\nreuses a pooled SCP/SFTP connection that was established by an earlier transfer under a weaker (or\nabsent) host-key policy. Host-key verification is curl's defence against a man-in-the-middle on an\nSSH transfer; bypassing it on reuse defeats MITM detection for the transfer that asked for it.\n\nRealistic shape: a process does an initial unverified SCP/SFTP operation (or one whose host-key was\naccepted with no known_hosts), then a second operation to the same host/user with a pin configured.\nThe second operation inherits the first connection without re-checking the host key, so an attacker\nwho was able to MITM the first (unverified) connection is not caught by the second transfer's pin.\n\n## What it is / is not\n\n- It **is** a host-key verification bypass on connection reuse — the SSH analogue of the TLS reuse\n  tightening in CVE-2022-27782, whose SSH portion only added the identity key-file comparison and\n  omitted the host-key verification inputs.\n- It **is** confirmed live end-to-end: a wrong host-key pin that rejects on a fresh connection\n  (rc=60) is silently accepted on a reused connection (rc=0). Code trace, live PoC, and both\n  controls are in `attachments/`.\n- It is **not** memory-unsafe.\n\n## Honest severity\n\nMedium, matching the parent CVE-2022-27782. The effect is a security-control bypass (host-key\nverification / MITM detection), not merely a behavioural inconsistency, which is why I rate it above\nthe other reuse-key gaps. It is now demonstrated at runtime, not only from the source.",
